HIRAX leaves: Maxwell scattering (MATLAB)
========================================

Purpose
  Build and apply a direct solver for the complete corrected four-leaf NRCCIE
  boundary-integral matrix A, then use it to assemble the scattering matrix.
  The production solve is

    A x = b,              A approximately F  (strong skeletonization),

  where F is one asymmetric RS-S factorization of the full four-leaf operator.

Code layout
  geometry/         leaf mesh generation, patch partitioning, and plotting
  quadrature/       compact C4 corrections, built in target-row batches
  solver/           selected matrix entries, FMM application, and RS-S solves
  skeletonization/  deterministic incoming/outgoing IDs and Scat assembly
  test/             the complete construction and independent field checks

  The main path is generate_leaves_surfer -> fourfold_quad_corr_mats ->
  nrccie_srskelf -> fourfold_nrccie_skeleton -> nrccie_scattering_matrix.
  nrccie_apply supplies the full-system FMM residual, and
  nrccie_scattering_field_checks validates the resulting exterior fields.

Dependencies
  Place fmm3dbie-hirax-dev, FLAM, chunkie, and strong-skel next to this
  repository. FMM3DBIE and FMM3D MEX files must already be compiled.
  Run the repository-root startup.m to set the MATLAB path. Use the HIRAX
  strong-skel fork, which supports the empty-proxy interface used here:

    https://github.com/wangyuguan/strong-skel

  See that dependency's README for the attributed RS-S implementation.

Direct factorization of A
  The geometry consists of four C4-related leaves in cycle [1 2 4 3]. Only one
  compact quadrature-correction matrix is stored for each relative C4
  orientation. The RS-S callback forms any requested submatrix A(rows,cols)
  from the analytic NRCCIE block plus the corresponding compact correction.
  It never forms the dense global matrix A.

  The RS-S tree uses XY patch centers because each leaf is thin in z; all
  kernels and quadrature remain fully three-dimensional. Strong
  skeletonization selects scalar unknowns, as required by the external RS-S
  package. The incoming and outgoing scattering spaces are instead selected
  by complete physical nodes, retaining all three [ju;jv;rho] components.

  Each right-hand side is first solved by an RS-S backsolve. The true residual
  is then evaluated with the original corrected operator A, using an FMM
  tolerance tighter than the requested solve tolerance. Residual-based
  iterative refinement applies additional RS-S backsolves until the requested
  global residual is reached or the explicit refinement limit is exhausted.

Incoming, outgoing, and Scat
  Incoming skeleton nodes interpolate sampled plane-wave boundary data.
  Outgoing skeleton nodes reproduce fields measured on a proxy surface.
  Both deterministic multilevel IDs are built on one representative leaf and
  rotated to the other three leaves under C4 symmetry. They define the input
  and output coordinates of Scat; they are independent of how A is solved.

  Scat is assembled in column batches. Every batch uses the same direct A
  factor and the same full-A residual check/refinement. There is no global
  recompression of the four rotated incoming or outgoing spaces.

Example
  Edit the numerical parameters near the top of
  leaves/test/nrccie_scattering_matrix_leaf.m, then run from the repository
  root, for example,

    matlab -batch \
      "maxNumCompThreads(24); run('leaves/test/nrccie_scattering_matrix_leaf.m')"

  All mesh, solver, skeleton, and field-check parameters are written directly
  in that script. C4 correction reuse is always enabled. The default leaf
  geometry uses wavelength 138.5 mm, approximately one nominal leaf width;
  the outgoing proxy is four wavelengths away. Incoming training uses 1024
  deterministic C4-closed directions with two polarizations. Held-out fields
  are checked at 7, 8, and 10 wavelengths.

  At norder=1 there are 2,952 nodes per leaf and 35,424 scalar unknowns in A.
  At norder=4 there are 17,400 nodes per leaf and 208,800 scalar unknowns.

Reference solve
  The optional plain reference is disabled by default. Set run_reference=true
  in the example to compare with zero-start, unrestarted, unpreconditioned
  full-system FMM/GMRES. The reference applies the same compact C4 quadrature
  corrections but does not use the RS-S factor. Disabling it does not disable
  Scat assembly, held-out residual checks, or finite-radius field checks.
  Agreement with this reference tests the discrete algebra; it is not a mesh-
  convergence study.

Outputs
  New results are saved under leaves/test/results/.

  Progress, skeleton sizes, true residuals, refinement counts, and timings
  are printed to the MATLAB console.

  progress.mat
    A small status record; it is not a restart checkpoint.

  factor.mat
    The reusable direct RS-S factor of the full corrected matrix A and its
    factorization metadata.

  result.mat
    Scat, incoming/outgoing skeleton surfaces, physical parameters, timing and
    error records, and held-out finite-radius fields.

  A successful complete run writes result.passed=true and prints
  NRCCIE_SCATTERING_MATRIX_LEAF_PASS. Each run must use a new output directory;
  existing results are not overwritten.

  A saved scattering matrix is applied as

    bin  = nrccie_planewave_rhs(Sin,zk,alpha,rotations);
    qout = Scat*bin;
    EH   = nrccie_matrix(Sout,targets,zk)*qout;

Parameters
  The main numerical tolerance is tol. The local RS-S IDs use this tolerance;
  the true-residual FMM evaluation uses a tighter tolerance. max_nodes controls
  leaf occupancy in the RS-S tree, max_refinement limits full-system residual
  corrections, scattering_batch_size controls Scat workspace, and maxiter is
  used only by the optional plain-GMRES reference. GMRES restart is not used.
